What each hunk of php-5.0.0-0003-zend-alloc-sublet-hooks.patch does, and why it is there.

The patch is INERT without -DZEND_CAP_SUBLET: the two macros then expand to exactly the
arithmetic they replace, and zend_alloc.c compiles to the same object as the pristine source.
The build asserts that with cmp rather than trusting it, so the physical column's results and
Phase A's hierarchical results are unaffected by the file having been patched at all.

Nothing in PHP's own logic moves. Not REAL_SIZE, not cache_index, not MAX_CACHED_MEMORY, not
the list macros, not the cache policy, not the control flow of any function. The patch adds no
statement to the file: it is seven substitutions of an expression.

  hunk 1  after the ZEND_DO_* macro chain
          Adds the seam's own block: the include of zend-cap-mode.h, the three ZEND_DO_*
          macros redirected to the seam's counted 16-grained malloc, its arm-dependent free
          and its move-always realloc, and the three ZEND_CAP_* macros with their inert
          fallbacks. This is the only hunk that adds more than one line of code.
          ZEND_DO_REALLOC reads the old request from p->size, which _erealloc overwrites only
          AFTER the realloc: that is the copy length of the fresh block.

  hunk 2  _emalloc, the AG(cache) HIT return
          (void *)((char *)p + sizeof(zend_mem_header) + MEM_HEADER_PADDING)
            -> ZEND_CAP_PAYLOAD(p, size)
          The cache path matters as much as the fresh one: a block handed back from the cache
          must be re-issued as a FRESH child, or the previous tenant's alias is still live over
          the same bytes. That is the one property an allocator reusing bytes inside a live
          outer allocation does not get for free.

  hunk 3  _emalloc, the FRESH return -- same substitution.

  hunk 4  _efree, the header derivation
          (zend_mem_header *)((char *)ptr - 48) -> ZEND_CAP_RECLAIM(ptr)
          By RECLAIM, not arithmetic: the caller's capability is bounded to its own object and
          cannot reach the header at all, which is what makes an under-read below the object
          catchable. The reclaim is the libc's (__capstone_malloc_reclaim): it ends the caller's
          child and returns the block from its own slot record. So the payload's lifetime ends in
          the FIRST line of _efree, and everything below it -- p->size, p->cached = 1, the park
          itself, REMOVE_POINTER_FROM_LIST and ZEND_DO_FREE -- runs against the block's own
          reference, live, exactly as upstream. An interior pointer, or one in no block of this
          heap, is refused by name before anything ends (ZEND_CAP_FAIL code 4 / 3).
          THIS IS WHERE THE PHYSICAL PORT HAD TO DEVIATE. With one capability spanning header
          and payload, revoking the caller's pointer killed the header too, so that port
          hoisted REMOVE_POINTER_FROM_LIST above the revoke and made it unconditional -- a
          change to PHP's own _efree that cost four false CAUGHT verdicts to find and still
          costs CRASH-057 a NO-READING. The child bounded to the payload makes it unnecessary.
          The previous seam (2026-10-12 to 2026-10-28) did the same with a lookup in its own
          index and a separate retire statement placed after CALCULATE_REAL_SIZE_AND_CACHE_INDEX;
          the libc's reclaim returns the block, so the statement is gone and the ordering is
          no longer a concern.

  hunk 5  _erealloc, the header derivation (p = orig = ...) -> ZEND_CAP_HEADER(ptr).
          The LOOKUP alone: nothing ends here. `orig` is load-bearing in upstream: the failure
          path does ADD_POINTER_TO_LIST(orig), so the old block must still be alive after a
          failed realloc, and so must the caller's pointer into it. ZEND_DO_REALLOC (hunk 1)
          takes a fresh block, copies, and releases the old one only then -- which, in the
          arms that return blocks to the heap, is what ends the caller's old child, with the
          block. On failure nothing has changed.

  hunk 6  _erealloc, the payload return -- same substitution as hunks 2 and 3. Note the
          upstream text here has no spaces around the + signs, which is why it is a separate
          hunk rather than part of one replacement.

  hunk 7  _mem_block_check, the header derivation -> ZEND_CAP_HEADER(ptr).
          NOT COMPILED in this configuration: lines 640-786 of the pristine file are inside
          #if ZEND_DEBUG, and ZEND_DEBUG is 0 here. Patched anyway so that "every derivation in
          this file goes through the hook" holds without a caveat.

NOT PATCHED, deliberately: line 778's payload derivation inside the same ZEND_DEBUG region
feeds _mem_block_check's argument and is dead for the same reason; and the ZEND_MM /
ZEND_WIN32 branches of the ZEND_DO_* chain, which this tree cannot take (Zend/zend_mm.h:31-36
undefines ZEND_MM unconditionally).
